=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router1
diagnosis_text:
The reported physical link failure between router0 and router1 does not exist.
Both sides of wan_link_r0_r1 (router0.eth2 at 10.16.1.1/24, router1.eth2 at
10.16.1.2/24) show link state UP/LOWER_UP, carry their correct IP addresses, and
pass a live ping with 0% packet loss. The L1/L2 link is entirely healthy.

The real fault is on router1: its RIP daemon (ripd) is not running. The FRR
service is reported as inactive/dead, watchfrr has failed (so there is no
automatic respawn), and the `show ip rip` vtysh command confirms "ripd is not
running." All per-daemon FRR config files on router1 are empty and no unified
frr.conf exists, meaning FRR started with a blank configuration and ripd
subsequently failed.

Because ripd is absent, router1 cannot participate in RIP route exchange. It
does not advertise its connected segments (10.16.1.0/24, 10.20.1.0/24) or
transit routes to its neighbours, and it does not learn any remote prefixes.
Hosts within each zone still reach their local gateway (connected routes are
unaffected) but cross-zone paths that must traverse router1 lose their RIP-
learned routes once the 180-second holddown expires, producing exactly the
symptom of partial inter-zone reachability loss.

Root cause: The ripd process was killed (or failed to start) on router1 and
watchfrr is also down, preventing automatic recovery.

Corrective fix: Restore a valid RIP configuration in /etc/frr/ripd.conf
(network 10.0.0.0/8 and any required LAN networks, version 2) and restart FRR
fully — `service frr restart` — so that watchfrr, zebra, and ripd all come up
together and router1 resumes advertising and learning routes.
=== END ===
```